  "narrative": "Current scientific report after the H183 validation response. H183 tests whether TP53 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, with the signed direction +1 meaning a positive estimate indicates weaker, less negative dependency. The discovery estimate R138 was +0.0157 with 95% interval -0.0247 to +0.0561. The independent validation estimate R156 was +0.0158 with adjusted interval -0.0464 to +0.0779. Both point estimates are above the approximate NFE2L2 clinical threshold of about 0.0093 and replicate in direction, but both confidence intervals include the null and admit clinically meaningful effects in either direction. The independent validation is therefore not confirmatory. The failure or weakness of more focused TP53-to-NFE2L2 follow-ups further argues against upgrading this signal: H204, the KEAP1-loss-restricted TP53 effect, was rejected after validation; H172, the high-quality Avana direct test, was essentially null; H206, the high-quality Avana TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction, was null but imprecise; and H225, low serum, was positive but imprecise. For these reasons H183 remains unresolved as a low-confidence residual association and its investigation is deferred. It should not be treated as an accepted TP53-to-NFE2L2 dependency program, but I do not reject it outright because the broad discovery and validation point estimates are consistent and above threshold. The main supported dataset-specific findings remain four large dependency programs. First, SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency: discovery R7 +0.784 and validation R117 +0.792, with robust replication across arid1a context, technical library, screen batch, adenocarcinoma, serum states, Cas9-quality strata, KEAP1 and TP53-intact contexts, and other selected subsets. Second, MSI-high status is associated with much stronger WRN dependency: discovery R9 +0.790 and validation R103 +0.822, with robust replication across BRCA2-intact, Avana, KRAS-intact, TP53-intact, KEAP1-intact, STK11-intact, adenocarcinoma, high-Cas9, Ky-library, and low-serum contexts. Third, KEAP1 loss is associated with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency: signed discovery R8/R14 +0.796 and validation R13 +0.810, with robustness across TP53, STK11, KRAS, adenocarcinoma, high-quality Avana, non-Avana, serum, and TP53-loss contexts. Fourth, TP53 loss is associated with much weaker USP7 dependency: discovery R23 +0.802 and validation R26 +0.804, with robustness across Avana, non-Avana, SMARCA4-intact, KEAP1-intact, KEAP1-loss, adenocarcinoma, high-quality Avana, serum states, and high Cas9-quality contexts. These are associations in the supplied lung-lineage dataset only; they do not establish causal mechanisms or external generalizability. The negative and unsupported evidence is extensive. Broad TP53 loss on KIF18A was rejected after validation collapsed the discovery effect, and selected high-quality adenocarcinoma KIF18A contexts were also rejected. The proposed KEAP1-loss-restricted TP53-to-NFE2L2 effect was rejected after independent validation. Broad or selected TP53 loss on MDM2 remained near null or failed validation. Driver amplification or mutation effects on their own or related dependency scores were generally not clinically precise, including KRAS mutation on KRAS dependency, EGFR amplification on EGFR dependency, ERBB2 amplification on ERBB2 dependency, MYC amplification on DHX9 or POLR2A, and many PIK3CA, PTEN, and NF1 refinement lines. Broad KEAP1 loss on USP7 did not overturn the accepted TP53-to-USP7 interpretation because KEAP1-USP7 estimates were near null or imprecise. Technical hypotheses such as Ky-library effects on POLR2A were rejected, and most lineage, research-marker, growth-rate, aneuploidy, EMV-like state-marker, and interaction modifiers did not provide robust clinically meaningful evidence. No validation requests remain. The most informative next analyses are discovery context and co-alteration mappings that can increase precision or define context dependence, especially selected claims already registered around accepted programs and unresolved TP53-NFE2L2 residuals. If future direct tests are to affect the TP53-NFE2L2 question, they should substantially improve precision or provide a coherent conditional pattern; another imprecise near-threshold estimate would add little. All conclusions should remain framed as dataset-specific associations rather than causal or generalizable biological claims.",
  "research_notes": "H183 response assessed: keep unresolved and deferred. R138 and R156 give replicated positive estimates above NFE2L2 clinical threshold but both intervals cross null; validation does not confirm, and focused TP53-NFE2L2 tests are null or failed. Do not accept H183 as TP53-to-NFE2L2 program; do not reject because replicated positive point estimates. Accepted dataset-specific association programs remain: SMARCA4 loss to much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high to much stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss to much weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 loss to much weaker USP7 dependency. Rejected or unsupported include broad TP53-KIF18A, KEAP1-restricted TP53-NFE2L2, selected MDM2/EGFR/PTEN/NF1/MYC/PIK3CA/PTEN lines, broad KEAP1-USP7 independent of TP53, self-driver amplification/mutation effects, research markers, Ky-POLR2A artifact, and most lineage/state/growth/aneuploidy interaction modifiers. No validation requests remain. Next analyze should select registered context/co-alteration claims with adequate cells to test robustness of accepted programs and only high-precision tests relevant to residual H183."
}
